     - \"Take the reactor of `test/v4-platform/maven-architecture.test.ts` and add `junit-vintage-engine` 5.10.2 to its test dependencies.\"\n      - \"Adopt the map, then qualify the architecture control with the network closed: it is not qualified, the run failing on the resolution of `junit-vintage-engine` 5.14.4.\"\n      - \"With `PROJECT_JUNIT_ENGINES = []`, the same qualification fails on a `NoClassDefFoundError` of misaligned JUnit jars.\"\n      - \"With `PROJECT_JUNIT_ENGINES = [\\\"org.junit.jupiter\\\"]`, the four tests of `test/v4-platform/maven-architecture.test.ts` stay green.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      Not confirmed by a probe. The profile puts the JUnit Platform 1.14.4 that ArchUnit is built on ahead of the\n      project's, and excludes the project's engines from the classpath; the version 5.14.4 that the closed network\n      refuses is the Vintage engine of that platform, which the Surefire of the profile appears to fetch when it\n      starts the tests because the project declares a Vintage engine, and which the resolution at adoption does not\n      fetch.\n    evidence: >\n      Round 1 of the review of e11s02, findings B1-2 and A1-2: a throwaway test on the reactor of\n      `maven-architecture.test.ts` plus `junit-vintage-engine` 5.10.2 failed with the code as it stands and with\n      `PROJECT_JUNIT_ENGINES = []`; the mutation dropping `org.junit.vintage` left the four tests green.\n    risk_level: medium\n    follow_ups: >\n      Make the resolution at adoption fetch whatever the rules' run needs on a reactor that declares the Vintage\n      engine, or keep the run from needing it, and add a platform test where a reactor with JUnit 4 tests run by\n      the Vintage engine has its control qualified and its project tests passing, which also holds the exclusion.\n    status: open\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-08T235300\n    date: \"2026-10-08\"\n    title: \"No test reads the English text of the outcome that adopts the architecture map with its verification\"\n    severity: low\n    priority: p3\n    scope: application\n    what_happened: >\n      Introduced by the branch `e11s02`. The outcome `adopt_map` of the decision that adopts the architecture map\n      (`ARCHITECTURE_MAP_ADOPTION`, `src/application/decisions.ts`) says, in French and in English, what adopting\n      the map has verified and that nothing is written in the project. The test of the scenario\n      (`test/v2-kernel/architecture-map-verification.test.ts`) asserts the French text alone: removing « the absence\n      of cycles and » or « ; nothing is written in the project » from the English text leaves it green, while the\n      same removal from the French text fails it.\n    what_expected: >\n      The story's scenario « La décision dit ce qu'adopter la carte fait vérifier, et par quoi » is held in both\n      languages of the decision: a test asserts the English outcome names the rules ArchUnit verifies and says that\n      nothing is written in the project.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"In the `en` outcome of `ARCHITECTURE_MAP_ADOPTION`, remove `the absence of cycles and `, then `; nothing is written in the project`.\"\n      - \"Run `node --test test/v2-kernel/architecture-map-verification.test.ts`: 3/3 pass.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      The test of the scenario was written for the default language of the session; the English half of the Then has\n      no assertion.\n    evidence: >\n      Round 1 of the review of e11s02, finding B1-3: both removals from the English text left the three tests of\n      `architecture-map-verification.test.ts` green.\n    risk_level: low\n    follow_ups: >\n      Assert the English outcome of `adopt_map` alongside the French one: the rules it names, the step with the\n      network open, and that nothing is written in the project.\n    status: open\nEOF\nnode -e 'const y=require(require.resolve(\"yaml\",{paths:[require.resolve(\"@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\")]}));const d=y.parse(require(\"fs\")